#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""
jen-kea-helper — the fixed-function privilege boundary on a Kea host
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
v5.11.0. Installed at /usr/local/sbin/jen-kea-helper, owned root:root
mode 0755. Jen (running as www-data on a *different* host) invokes it
over SSH as:

    sudo -n /usr/local/sbin/jen-kea-helper <op>

with ONE JSON object on stdin and gets ONE JSON object back on stdout.
Nothing else is ever written to stdout; a single diagnostic line goes
to stderr per call.

Why this exists
───────────────
Before 5.11.0 every Kea-side operation Jen performs — editing a subnet,
authoring a config, restarting a daemon, reading a log, installing a
package — was done by generating a Python script on the Jen host,
base64-ing it, piping it over SSH into `sudo python3`, and running it
as root. The documented Kea-side sudoers grant was therefore
`NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/python3`, i.e. **root**. A compromised www-data on
the Jen host was root on every Kea host it manages.

This helper replaces that with a closed set of operations behind ONE
sudoers line:

    youruser ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/jen-kea-helper

The bare command (no argument list) means "any arguments allowed" —
that is deliberate: the control is *this file's* op allowlist and path
walls, not sudo's argument matching. www-data cannot read or modify
this file, and the helper never executes anything it is handed: stdin
is data only. There is still no `self-update` op in the sense of
"install whatever Jen sends" — that capability is exactly what would
put "Jen writes a root-executed file on the Kea host" straight back.
From v6 (v5.66.0, Q103) there IS an `update` op, but it installs a
candidate ONLY when `ssh-keygen -Y verify` accepts a signature from the
Jen *project's* release key (never Jen's own SSH identity) under a
dedicated namespace, and only when the candidate declares a strictly
higher `HELPER_VERSION` — see that op's own section below. A helper
below v6 has no such op; updating it still needs a manual copy by an
administrator through the legacy grant, for that one last hop; see
docs/admin-guide.md "Kea host helper".

The legacy `sudo python3` path stays in Jen as a banner-warned fallback
for hosts that don't have the helper yet, and is never removed in the
5.x line (removing it would break a clean upgrade → a MAJOR change).

Protocol
────────
- stdin: one JSON object (an empty object is allowed). > 4 MiB → refused.
- stdout: exactly one JSON object, always with an `"ok"` boolean.
- exit 0 when the protocol worked (even if `"ok": false`, e.g. a config
  that failed `kea-dhcp4 -t`); exit 2 for protocol misuse (unknown op,
  unreadable JSON, oversize stdin) — still printing a JSON object.
- `HELPER_VERSION` is the protocol version. Bump it only when an op's
  request/response contract changes; Jen refuses a helper older than
  its `JEN_HELPER_MIN_VERSION`. `HELPER_BUILD` (v7, Q104) is a separate,
  plain integer bumped on EVERY change to this file, never reset by a
  version bump — `HELPER_VERSION` alone can't order two files that
  changed without a protocol change between them.

v2 (v5.16.0) — optimistic concurrency:
- `read-config` responses gain `"sha256"` — the hex SHA-256 of the raw
  file *bytes* (whitespace included), so Jen can tell if the file was
  hand-edited since a form was opened.
- `apply-config` accepts an optional `"expect_sha256"`. When present the
  op takes an exclusive `flock` on `<path>.jen_lock` (held through the
  replace), compares the current file's SHA against it BEFORE running
  `kea-dhcpX -t`, and returns `{"ok": false, "error": "conflict",
  "sha256": <current>}` on a mismatch — nothing written. `""` means
  "must not exist". Every `apply-config` response now carries the SHA of
  the bytes it just wrote.
- Every response envelope carries `"helper_version"`.

v3 (v5.23.0) — `"d2"` (kea-dhcp-ddns, Kea's DNS-update daemon) joins
`"dhcp4"`/`"dhcp6"` as a valid `service` everywhere one is accepted:
`read-config`/`test-config`/`apply-config` resolve it to the
`kea-dhcp-ddns` binary and (Jen-side) `kea-dhcp-ddns.conf`; `service`
resolves its unit to `kea-dhcp-ddns-server` / `isc-kea-dhcp-ddns-server`.
No protocol shape changed — a v2 caller talking to a v3 helper sees
identical behavior for dhcp4/dhcp6; a v2 helper simply refuses `"d2"`
as `not-allowed`, since `_valid_service` on that host doesn't know it
yet, which is why this is a version bump and not silently backward
compatible for the new capability.

v4 (v5.29.0) — `install-tls`: writes the TLS material for one daemon's
https control socket — exactly three files, `ca.crt`, `server.crt`,
`server.key`, under the FIXED directory `/etc/kea/tls/<service>/`. The
payload names the service and carries the three PEM bodies; it never
carries a path. The directory is created `root:root 0755`; the files are
written tmp + `os.replace`, owned `root:<daemon group>` (the unit's
`User=`, else `_kea`, else root), mode `0640` for the key and `0644` for
the certificates; a pre-planted symlink at the root, the directory, or
any target is refused. Content is guarded only by PEM block markers and
a 64 KiB cap — the helper is pure stdlib and cannot parse PEM; Jen
validates the material it generated before sending it. A v3 helper
answers `unknown-op`, which Jen turns into "needs helper v4"; the op is
never routed through the legacy `sudo python3` engine.

v5 (v5.49.0) — `tail-log` reads the file through a bounded
`collections.deque(f, maxlen=lines)` instead of `f.read().splitlines()`:
memory is bounded by the requested (at most 1000) lines whatever the size of
the log. The request and response shapes are unchanged; the bump exists so
Jen can offer the update (Settings → Kea → SSH shows "v5 available").

v6 (v5.66.0, Q103) — `update`: the ONE way this helper ever replaces itself.
A maintainer report: every helper update needed the legacy `NOPASSWD:
/usr/bin/python3` grant (real root) added by hand for one click, then
removed again — real friction, and the exact capability §3.3 exists to deny.
This closes it WITHOUT reopening "Jen writes a file the Kea host runs as
root": the candidate is installed only when it carries a valid
`ssh-keygen -Y verify` signature from the Jen project's own release key
(under a namespace, `jen-kea-helper`, distinct from the release-tarball
namespace `jen-release` — a checksum signature can never be replayed here or
vice versa) AND declares a STRICTLY higher `HELPER_VERSION` than this one.
A fully-compromised Jen can therefore install a genuine, newer Jen release's
helper and nothing else — the same trust root as the self-updater
(docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §3.1, §3.3). The op never touches sudoers (the
helper's own sudoers line is unaffected either way) and never restarts
anything. `RELEASE_SIGNERS` here is byte-identical to the same-named
constant in `jen-update-root.py` (a test enforces it) — the local
`/etc/jen-kea-helper/allowed_signers` file (root-owned, not group/other
writable, ≤ 8 KiB) is an OPTIONAL addition on top of it, for key rotation
and test doubles, never a replacement.

v7 (v5.66.0-beta.2, Q104) — a third-party review of the v6 design (verdict:
"fundamentally good and should remain") found the hardening below still
missing. None of it changes what `update` VERIFIES — only how carefully
everything around it behaves:
- **No PATH trust.** Every binary this helper ever runs (`kea-dhcp4/6`,
  `kea-dhcp-ddns`, `systemctl`, `apt-get`, `ssh-keygen`) is resolved through
  `_find_bin()`, a root-owned allowlist walk over `_BIN_DIRS` — never
  `shutil.which()`, never a bare name handed to `subprocess.run`. The
  process itself now runs `#!/usr/bin/python3 -I` (isolated mode: no
  `PYTHON*` env vars, no script directory on `sys.path`) and every
  subprocess it spawns gets a fixed, minimal environment. `sudo -n
  /usr/local/sbin/jen-kea-helper` means the kernel resolves the shebang and
  every child process through sudo's own `secure_path` today, and that is
  genuinely safe on the targets this ships to — but a root privilege
  boundary must not depend on the caller's sudoers configuration staying
  that way forever.
- **`HELPER_BUILD`.** `HELPER_VERSION` is the protocol/capability number,
  bumped only when a request/response contract changes — but a helper-only
  fix (like the PATH hardening above) changes the FILE without changing
  the protocol, and `op_update`'s old `new_version <= HELPER_VERSION` check
  would call that `not-newer` forever. `HELPER_BUILD` is a plain integer,
  bumped on every change to this file, never reset by a version bump;
  `update` (from v7 on) requires the candidate's `HELPER_BUILD` to be
  greater than this file's own (equal or lower → `not-newer`), and its
  `HELPER_VERSION` to be at least this file's own (a protocol DOWNGRADE is
  still never installed, even signed, even with a higher build).
- **Preflight and rollback.** A signed, strictly-newer candidate could
  still have a syntax error, a Python-version incompatibility, or a
  host-specific import failure — `update` used to write and `os.replace`
  it with no check beyond "the bytes verify". Now, after the signature and
  version/build checks: the candidate is preflighted by running its own
  `version` op in a throwaway process (`/usr/bin/python3 -I <candidate>
  version` — the same fixed interpreter path every target has, exactly the
  path the legacy grant names — with an empty JSON object on stdin, a
  10 s timeout, the same fixed environment) and requiring exit 0 plus a
  JSON reply whose `helper_version`/`helper_build` match what the
  candidate declared; only then is it fsynced, the CURRENT helper copied
  aside to `<_SELF_PATH>.prev`, and `os.replace()`d in. A POSTFLIGHT repeats
  the same check against the newly-installed path — a failure there
  restores `.prev` over `_SELF_PATH` and reports `postflight-failed`
  instead of leaving a broken helper live; success removes `.prev`.

Build 8 (v5.66.0-beta.4, Q106) — a third-party review of v7 found the one
path through `op_update` that installs bytes with no way back: with no
regular file at `_SELF_PATH` (a rare state — the running helper normally
IS that file — but the postflight rollback above only fires `if
prev_saved`, and `prev_saved` was False here), the old code still ran the
replace, so a postflight failure on that first "update" had nothing to
restore. `update` replaces an installed helper, it never installs one:
`op_update` now refuses BEFORE writing or preflighting any candidate
bytes, with `not-installed`, whenever `_SELF_PATH` is not a plain regular
file. That makes the `.prev` copy unconditional — `prev_saved` is gone as
a variable — so a rollback is always attempted on a postflight failure,
and a rollback that ITSELF fails (`os.replace` raising, e.g. disk full)
is reported as `rollback-failed`, naming both `_SELF_PATH` and its
`.prev`, instead of the misleading `postflight-failed` (which implies the
old helper is back). This is the first build-only change: `HELPER_BUILD`
moves to 8 with `HELPER_VERSION` unchanged at 7 — the two new error
tokens are values inside the existing envelope, not a contract change.

Build 9 (v5.66.0-beta.7, Q109) — ChatGPT's review found a real gap in the ordering check
itself: `op_update` refused a candidate as `not-newer` only when `new_version <
HELPER_VERSION`, or (nested under `new_version == HELPER_VERSION`) when `new_build <=
HELPER_BUILD` — so a candidate declaring a HIGHER `HELPER_VERSION` skipped the build check
entirely, letting a lower build through on a protocol bump (a build-only fix accidentally
regressed by whatever built the higher-versioned candidate). The two checks are now
INDEPENDENT, run unconditionally in this order: `new_version < HELPER_VERSION` refuses with
detail `protocol-downgrade`; `new_build <= HELPER_BUILD` refuses with detail
`build-not-newer` — regardless of how the versions compare. `kea_host.py`'s own
`install_helper()` "already installed" short-circuit follows the identical rule now (it used
to compare `(version, build)` as a single lexicographic tuple, which had the same gap).
`_find_bin()` also gained a directory-level check, `_bin_dir_ok()`: the DIRECTORY a trusted
binary lives in (its `realpath`, so a usrmerge `/bin -> /usr/bin` symlink is followed) must
itself be root-owned and not group/other-writable before any file inside it is even looked
at — defence in depth alongside the existing per-file `_bin_owner_ok()` check.

Build 10 (v5.68.0-beta.6, Q141) — the root:root rule of `_bin_owner_ok()` was applied to the
Kea DAEMON binary too, and ISC's own packages do not ship it that way: on a Kea 3.0.4 ISC
deb (`3.0.4 isc20260728182757 deb`) `/usr/sbin/kea-dhcp4` is `_kea:_kea`, mode 0750. Every op
that validates a config (`test-config` and `apply-config` both go through `_run_kea_test`)
answered `missingbinary` there, which Jen worded "kea-dhcp4 is not installed on this server" -
so no config change reached Kea through the helper from build 7 (v5.66.0-beta.2) until this
build. The test is now run AS THE ACCOUNT THE DAEMON RUNS AS, and trust follows who executes:
a binary the helper runs as root must still be root:root with no group/other write bit
(nothing else this helper runs changes); the Kea daemon binary is run through
`subprocess.run(..., user=, group=)` as the unit's `User=`, else the binary's own owner, and
is trusted only when it is a regular file owned by exactly that system account (a uid below
1000, never root) with no group/other write bit - what the daemon itself does on every start,
so a compromised `_kea`-owned binary gains nothing it did not already have, and the helper
(root) never executes it. Anything else is still `missingbinary`, now with the reason in
`detail` ("owned by alice, not the daemon's user"). The temp file `-t` reads is written 0644
explicitly (it used to rely on the umask) and `_CLEAN_ENV` gains `HOME=/` for the
dropped-privilege run. A change of behaviour inside the existing envelope, not a contract
change: `HELPER_VERSION` stays 7.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import base64
import binascii
import collections
import hashlib
import json
import os
import posixpath
import re
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess  # nosec B404 — every call is a fixed argv list, shell=False
import sys
import tempfile

HELPER_VERSION = 7
HELPER_BUILD = 10
# v5.66.0-beta.2 (Q104) — HELPER_BUILD is bumped on EVERY change to this file, never by
# HELPER_VERSION alone; op_update's own _HELPER_BUILD_RE parses "^HELPER_BUILD = (\d+)$" out
# of a candidate's raw bytes, so this line (like HELPER_VERSION's own, just above) must never
# carry a trailing comment — anything after the digit breaks that end-of-line anchor and a
# genuine candidate would come back "unparseable".

MAX_STDIN = 4 * 1024 * 1024  # 4 MiB

# v5.66.0-beta.2 (Q104) — the root-owned binary allowlist every subprocess call resolves
# through, in this fixed order, NEVER $PATH. os.environ["PATH"] is under sudo's control
# today (Ubuntu's own secure_path), which is safe now but not something a root privilege
# boundary should depend on staying true. /usr/local/bin is where the system suite's
# systemctl/keactl stand-ins live (tests/system/compose/kea-node/Dockerfile).
_BIN_DIRS = ("/usr/sbin", "/usr/bin", "/sbin", "/bin", "/usr/local/sbin", "/usr/local/bin")
# The fixed environment every resolved binary runs under — never the inherited one. Ops
# that need an extra variable (apt-get's DEBIAN_FRONTEND) add it on top of this, per call.
_CLEAN_ENV = {"PATH": "/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin", "LANG": "C.UTF-8", "HOME": "/"}

# v4 — where `install-tls` writes, and the only names it writes. Fixed
# here, never taken from the payload: the op's whole path wall is "these
# three basenames under this one directory per service".
_TLS_ROOT = "/etc/kea/tls"
_TLS_FILES = ("ca.crt", "server.crt", "server.key")
_PEM_MAX = 64 * 1024
_PEM_CERTS_RE = re.compile(
    r"\A(?:-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\r?\n[A-Za-z0-9+/=\r\n]+-----END CERTIFICATE-----\r?\n?)+\Z"
)
_PEM_KEY_RE = re.compile(
    r"\A-----BEGIN (?:EC |RSA )?PRIVATE KEY-----\r?\n[A-Za-z0-9+/=\r\n]+-----END (?:EC |RSA )?PRIVATE KEY-----\r?\n?\Z"
)

# Directories a Kea config file may live in. Anything else is refused,
# independent of whatever path Jen sent — Jen validates its own copy,
# but this helper does not trust that.
_ALLOWED_CONF_DIRS = ("/etc/kea", "/usr/local/etc/kea")
_CONF_BASENAME_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+\.conf$")

_ALLOWED_SERVICES = ("dhcp4", "dhcp6", "d2")

# service -> (binary name, systemd unit family). d2's unit family is
# "dhcp-ddns", not "d2" — Kea's own package/unit naming, not Jen's service
# vocabulary.
_SERVICE_BINARY = {"dhcp4": "kea-dhcp4", "dhcp6": "kea-dhcp6", "d2": "kea-dhcp-ddns"}
_SERVICE_UNIT_FAM = {"dhcp4": "dhcp4", "dhcp6": "dhcp6", "d2": "dhcp-ddns"}


# ── Path walls ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


def _allowed_conf_path(p):
    """True iff `p` is an absolute, already-normalised path to a
    `*.conf` file directly inside one of the allowed Kea config dirs.
    posixpath, not os.path — these are always remote Linux paths."""
    if not isinstance(p, str) or not p:
        return False
    if not p.startswith("/") or posixpath.normpath(p) != p:
        return False
    if ".." in p.split("/"):
        return False
    if posixpath.dirname(p) not in _ALLOWED_CONF_DIRS:
        return False
    return bool(_CONF_BASENAME_RE.match(posixpath.basename(p)))


def _allowed_log_path(p):
    """True iff `p` is an absolute, already-normalised `*.log` path under
    /var/log (with symlinks in the existing prefix resolved)."""
    if not isinstance(p, str) or not p:
        return False
    if not p.startswith("/") or posixpath.normpath(p) != p:
        return False
    if not p.endswith(".log"):
        return False
    real = os.path.realpath(p)
    return real == "/var/log" or real.startswith("/var/log/")


def _valid_service(service):
    return service in _ALLOWED_SERVICES


def _file_sha256(path):
    """Hex SHA-256 of a file's raw bytes, or None if it can't be read."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            return hashlib.sha256(f.read()).hexdigest()
    except OSError:
        return None


def _bin_owner_ok(path):
    """True iff `path` is a REGULAR file, owned root:root, with no group/other write bit —
    the same shape as _extra_signers_owner_ok below, in its own function so a test (whose
    stub binaries are never really root-owned, since the test itself doesn't run as root)
    can monkeypatch it directly instead of faking filesystem ownership."""
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except OSError:
        return False
    if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
        return False
    return st.st_uid == 0 and st.st_gid == 0 and not (st.st_mode & 0o022)


def _bin_dir_ok(d):
    """True iff `d`'s REAL directory — os.path.realpath, so a usrmerge symlink like
    /bin -> /usr/bin is followed to the directory that actually holds the files — is owned
    by uid 0 and not group/other-writable (v5.66.0-beta.7, Q109). A per-FILE check alone
    (_bin_owner_ok, above) trusts whatever directory a candidate binary happens to sit in;
    this closes that gap one level up, before any file inside the directory is even looked
    at. In its own monkeypatchable function for the same reason _bin_owner_ok is."""
    try:
        st = os.stat(os.path.realpath(d))
    except OSError:
        return False
    return stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode) and st.st_uid == 0 and not (st.st_mode & 0o022)


def _find_bin(name):
    """The first candidate under _BIN_DIRS (that fixed order) whose directory passes
    _bin_dir_ok AND is itself a regular file, root-owned, and not group/other-writable —
    every binary this helper ever runs is resolved this way, NEVER through $PATH
    (v5.66.0-beta.2, Q104; the directory-level check is v5.66.0-beta.7, Q109)."""
    for d in _BIN_DIRS:
        if not _bin_dir_ok(d):
            continue
        cand = os.path.join(d, name)
        if _bin_owner_ok(cand) and os.access(cand, os.X_OK):
            return cand
    return None


def _run_bin(name, args, **kw):
    """Resolve `name` through _find_bin and run it with the fixed _CLEAN_ENV — never the
    inherited environment, never $PATH. `args` is the argument list AFTER the binary name.
    Returns a subprocess.CompletedProcess, or None if the binary could not be found at all
    under _BIN_DIRS (the caller decides what that means for its own op)."""
    binary = _find_bin(name)
    if binary is None:
        return None
    return subprocess.run([binary, *args], env=_CLEAN_ENV, **kw)  # nosec B603


def _unit_account(service):
    """(name, uid, gid) of the account the daemon's systemd unit runs as - its `User=` - or None
    when the unit sets none, names root, or the account does not exist here (v5.68.0-beta.6, Q141).
    Never raises: a host without systemd or `pwd` simply has no answer."""
    unit = _resolve_unit(service, "status")
    if not unit:
        return None
    proc = _run_bin("systemctl", ["show", "-p", "User", "--value", unit], capture_output=True, text=True)
    name = (proc.stdout or "").strip() if proc else ""
    if not name or name == "root":
        return None
    try:
        import pwd
    except ImportError:  # not a POSIX host (only ever the test harness)
        return None
    try:
        pw = pwd.getpwnam(name)
    except KeyError:
        return None
    return (name, pw.pw_uid, pw.pw_gid) if pw.pw_uid != 0 else None


def _daemon_bin_ok(path, uid):
    """True iff `path` is a REGULAR file owned by `uid`, a SYSTEM account (a uid below 1000, never
    root), with no group/other write bit - the only shape of Kea daemon binary the helper will run
    as that account (v5.68.0-beta.6, Q141). In its own function so a test, whose stub binaries are
    never really owned by a system account, can monkeypatch it the way _bin_owner_ok is."""
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except OSError:
        return False
    if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) or st.st_mode & 0o022:
        return False
    return st.st_uid == uid and 0 < uid < 1000


def _refusal_reason(path, unit_account):
    """Why a candidate that is not root:root is not trusted to run as the daemon's user - one
    sentence for the `detail` of `missingbinary` (v5.68.0-beta.6, Q141). Names facts, never paths
    other than the binary's own."""
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except OSError:
        return "cannot be read"
    if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
        return "is not a regular file"
    if st.st_mode & 0o022:
        return "is writable by its group or by everyone"
    try:
        import pwd

        owner = pwd.getpwuid(st.st_uid).pw_name
    except (ImportError, KeyError):
        owner = f"uid {st.st_uid}"
    if st.st_uid == 0:
        return "is owned by root but not root:root, and the helper runs it as the daemon's user only if that user owns it"
    if st.st_uid >= 1000:
        return f"is owned by {owner}, not a system account and not the daemon's user"
    if unit_account is not None:
        return f"is owned by {owner}, not the daemon's user {unit_account[0]}"
    return f"is owned by {owner}, which cannot be used as the daemon's user"


def _find_kea(service):
    """Resolve the Kea daemon binary and WHO runs it (v5.68.0-beta.6, Q141). Returns None when no
    candidate exists under _BIN_DIRS at all, else a dict: {"binary", "user", "group"} on success -
    user/group None means "run as root" (a root:root binary, the old rule, unchanged) - or
    {"refused": reason} when a candidate exists but is not trusted. Candidates are looked at in
    _BIN_DIRS order in directories that pass _bin_dir_ok; the first trusted one wins."""
    name = _SERVICE_BINARY.get(service, "kea-dhcp6")
    refused = None
    unit_account = None
    unit_looked = False
    for d in _BIN_DIRS:
        if not _bin_dir_ok(d):
            continue
        cand = os.path.join(d, name)
        if not os.path.lexists(cand):
            continue
        if _bin_owner_ok(cand) and os.access(cand, os.X_OK):
            return {"binary": cand, "user": None, "group": None}
        if not unit_looked:
            unit_account, unit_looked = _unit_account(service), True
        try:
            import pwd

            owner = pwd.getpwuid(os.lstat(cand).st_uid)
        except (ImportError, KeyError, OSError):
            owner = None
        # who the daemon runs as: its unit's User=, else the file's own owner
        run_uid, run_gid = (unit_account[1], unit_account[2]) if unit_account else (None, None)
        if run_uid is None and owner is not None:
            run_uid, run_gid = owner.pw_uid, owner.pw_gid
        if run_uid is not None and _daemon_bin_ok(cand, run_uid) and os.access(cand, os.X_OK):
            return {"binary": cand, "user": run_uid, "group": run_gid}
        if refused is None:
            refused = _refusal_reason(cand, unit_account)
    return {"refused": refused} if refused is not None else None


def _kea_binary(service):
    """Absolute path to the kea-dhcp4/6/dhcp-ddns binary a root-run or daemon-run test would use, or
    None - kept for callers that only need the path; the run itself goes through _find_kea."""
    found = _find_kea(service)
    return found.get("binary") if found else None


# ── Kea config test (shared by test-config and apply-config) ─────────────────


def _run_kea_test(service, path, config, tls_paths):
    """Write `config` to `<path>.jen_tmp` (0644, so the daemon's own account can read it), verify
    any TLS material exists, run `kea-dhcpX -t` against the temp file AS THE ACCOUNT THE DAEMON RUNS
    AS (`_find_kea`; root only for a root:root binary), and ALWAYS remove the temp file. Returns
    (ok: bool, result: dict). This only validates - apply re-writes its own."""
    found = _find_kea(service)
    name = _SERVICE_BINARY.get(service, "kea-dhcp6")
    if found is None or "binary" not in found:
        result = {"ok": False, "error": "missingbinary", "binary": name}
        if found and found.get("refused"):
            result["detail"] = found["refused"]
        return False, result
    binary = found["binary"]

    tmp = path + ".jen_tmp"
    try:
        fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o644)
        try:
            os.fchmod(fd, 0o644)  # whatever the umask said: the daemon's account must be able to read it
        except (OSError, AttributeError):
            pass
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
            json.dump(config, f, indent=2)

        for entry in tls_paths or []:
            tp, kind = entry[0], (entry[1] if len(entry) > 1 else "file")
            present = os.path.isdir(tp) if kind == "dir" else os.path.isfile(tp)
            if not present:
                return False, {"ok": False, "error": "tlsmissing", "path": tp}

        kw = {}
        if found.get("user") is not None:
            # the daemon's own account, no supplementary groups: what `kea-dhcpX` itself runs as on every start
            kw = {"user": found["user"], "group": found["group"], "extra_groups": []}
        proc = subprocess.run([binary, "-t", tmp], capture_output=True, text=True, env=_CLEAN_ENV, **kw)  # nosec B603
        combined = (proc.stdout or "") + (proc.stderr or "")
        if proc.returncode != 0 or "ERROR" in combined:
            lines = [ln for ln in combined.splitlines() if "ERROR" in ln or "Error" in ln]
            return False, {"ok": False, "error": "testerror", "detail": " | ".join(lines[:3])}
        return True, {"ok": True}
    finally:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass


